\begin_body
\begin_layout Title
Advanced Topics in Algorithms
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Lectures 1-3: Negative-Weight Shortest Paths
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Ron Goldman
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset FormulaMacro
\newcommand{\sset}[2]{\left\{  #1\middle|#2\right\}  }
{\left\{ #1|#2\right\} }
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset toc
LatexCommand tableofcontents

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Negative-weight single-source shortest paths
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We are given a directed graph 
\begin_inset Formula $G=(V,E,w)$
\end_inset

 with integer edge weights, possibly negative, and a source 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

. Two recent results:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Bernstein, Nanongkai, Wulff-Nilsen: Negative-weight single-source shortest paths in near-linear time (FOCS 2022, JACM 2025).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Bringmann, Cassis, Fischer: Negative-weight single-source shortest paths in near-linear time: now faster! (FOCS 2023).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
There are further follow-ups by now, also deterministic ones.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
\begin_inset Argument 1
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Bringmann et al.
\end_layout

\end_inset

There is a Las Vegas algorithm which, given a directed graph 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 with integer weights 
\begin_inset Formula $w(e)\geq-3W$
\end_inset

 and a source 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, either computes a shortest path tree from 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 or finds a negative cycle in 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

, in expected time 
\begin_inset Formula $O(\mathrm{Dijkstra}(G)\cdot\log^{2}n\cdot\log(nW))$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{Dijkstra}(G)=O(m+n\log n)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In these lectures we prove a slightly weaker version. The presentation follows Fischer et al., "A simple parallel algorithm with near-linear work for negative-weight single-source shortest paths" (SOSA 2025).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
There is a Las Vegas algorithm which, given a directed graph 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 without negative cycles, with integer weights 
\begin_inset Formula $w(e)\geq-3W$
\end_inset

, and a source 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, computes a shortest path tree from 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 in expected time 
\begin_inset Formula $O(\mathrm{Dijkstra}(G)\cdot\log^{5}n\cdot\log(nW))$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The classical algorithms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Dijkstra: only for nonnegative weights; 
\begin_inset Formula $O(m+n\log n)$
\end_inset

 with Fibonacci heaps.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Bellman-Ford: general weights, detects a negative cycle; 
\begin_inset Formula $O(mn)$
\end_inset

. It can stop early if the shortest paths contain few edges.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Algorithm
Dijkstra
\begin_inset Formula $(G,s)$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $d_{v}\leftarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in V$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{s}\leftarrow0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q\leftarrow\mathrm{PriorityQueue}(\{d_{s}:s\})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
While 
\begin_inset Formula $Q\neq\emptyset$
\end_inset

: pop 
\begin_inset Formula $u\in Q$
\end_inset

 with minimal 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{u}$
\end_inset

; for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,v)\in E$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
if 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{u}+w(u,v)<d_{v}$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{v}\leftarrow d_{u}+w(u,v)$
\end_inset

 and add/update 
\begin_inset Formula $(d_{v}:v)$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Algorithm
Bellman-Ford
\begin_inset Formula $(G,s)$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $d_{v}\leftarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in V$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{s}\leftarrow0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n-1$
\end_inset

: for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,v)\in E$
\end_inset

: if 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{u}+w(u,v)<d_{v}$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{v}\leftarrow d_{u}+w(u,v)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection
Combining Bellman-Ford and Dijkstra
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The algorithm alternates a Dijkstra phase on the nonnegative edges with a Bellman-Ford phase on the negative edges.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Algorithm
Bellman-Ford-Dijkstra
\begin_inset Formula $(G,s)$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $d_{v}\leftarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in V$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{s}\leftarrow0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q\leftarrow\mathrm{PriorityQueue}(\{d_{s}:s\})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
While 
\begin_inset Formula $Q\neq\emptyset$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $A\leftarrow\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Dijkstra phase on the nonnegative edges: while 
\begin_inset Formula $Q\neq\emptyset$
\end_inset

, pop 
\begin_inset Formula $u\in Q$
\end_inset

 with minimal 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{u}$
\end_inset

, set 
\begin_inset Formula $A\leftarrow A\cup\{u\}$
\end_inset

, and for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,v)\in E$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $w(u,v)\geq0$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
if 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{u}+w(u,v)<d_{v}$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{v}\leftarrow d_{u}+w(u,v)$
\end_inset

 and add/update 
\begin_inset Formula $(d_{v}:v)$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Bellman-Ford phase on the negative edges: for each 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in A$
\end_inset

 and edge 
\begin_inset Formula $(v,x)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $w(v,x)<0$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
if 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{v}+w(v,x)<d_{x}$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{x}\leftarrow d_{v}+w(v,x)$
\end_inset

 and add 
\begin_inset Formula $(d_{x}:x)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
To analyze it, let 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{i}(v)$
\end_inset

 be the length of a shortest path to 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 with at most 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 negative edges, and 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{i}^{+1}(v)$
\end_inset

 the length of a shortest path with at most 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 negative edges followed possibly by one last negative edge.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim
Invariants, by induction on the iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 of the outer loop:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
At the end of the Dijkstra phase of iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{v}=d_{i}(v)$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $A=\sset v{d_{i}(v)<d_{i-1}(v)}$
\end_inset

 (for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=0$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $A=\sset v{d_{0}(v)<\infty}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
At the end of the Bellman-Ford phase of iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{v}=d_{i}^{+1}(v)$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $Q=\sset v{d_{i}^{+1}(v)<d_{i}(v)}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim
\begin_inset Argument 1
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
running time
\end_layout

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{i}$
\end_inset

 be the set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 at the end of iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta(v)$
\end_inset

 be the minimum number of negative edges on a shortest path to 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

. Iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 takes 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{v\in A_{i}}(\deg(v)+\log n)$
\end_inset

 time, so the total running time is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
O\Big(\sum_{i}\sum_{v\in A_{i}}(\deg(v)+\log n)\Big)=O\Big(\sum_{v}(\deg(v)+\log n)(\eta(v)+1)\Big).
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A vertex enters 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{i}$
\end_inset

 only if its distance improves in iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, which happens at most 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta(v)+1$
\end_inset

 times.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We will use the algorithm with early stopping: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{BFD}(G,V,t)$
\end_inset

 starts with 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{v}\leftarrow0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 (all vertices in 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

, as from a super-source) and runs the outer loop at most 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 times (
\begin_inset Formula $j\leftarrow0$
\end_inset

; while 
\begin_inset Formula $Q\neq\emptyset$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j\leq t$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $j\leftarrow j+1$
\end_inset

). Its running time is 
\begin_inset Formula $O(\mathrm{Dijkstra}(G)\cdot t)=O\big((m+n\log n)\cdot t\big)$
\end_inset

, and it computes correctly every distance realized by a path with at most 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 negative edges.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Potentials
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi:V\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 let 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{\phi}(u,v)=w(u,v)+\phi(u)-\phi(v)$
\end_inset

. On every path from 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 the weight changes by exactly 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi(a)-\phi(b)$
\end_inset

, so shortest paths are the same under 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{\phi}$
\end_inset

, and cycles keep their weight.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
There is 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{\phi}(e)\geq0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 iff there is no negative cycle (that is, iff shortest paths are well defined).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Given such 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

, we can run Dijkstra on 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{\phi}$
\end_inset

 and compute the shortest paths.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Given 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{dist}(s,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 from a super-source, 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi(v)=\mathrm{dist}(s,v)$
\end_inset

 satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{\phi}(u,v)=w(u,v)+\phi(u)-\phi(v)\geq0$
\end_inset

 (triangle inequality).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So computing a valid potential is equivalent to computing SSSP, up to one run of Dijkstra.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Restricted graphs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A graph is restricted if its weights are integers 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq-1$
\end_inset

 and every cycle has mean weight at least 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

, that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $w(C)\geq|C|$
\end_inset

 for every cycle 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

. In a restricted graph we may assume all weights are 
\begin_inset Formula $<n-1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
\begin_inset Argument 1
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Restricted SSSP
\end_layout

\end_inset

In a restricted graph 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 we can compute a potential 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{\phi}(u,v)\geq0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,v)\in E$
\end_inset

 in expected time 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{r}(m,n)=O(\mathrm{Dijkstra}(G)\cdot\log^{5}n)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Scaling: from the general case to restricted graphs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assume now that 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 has no negative cycles and 
\begin_inset Formula $w(e)>-3W$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma
There is an algorithm 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{Scale}(G)$
\end_inset

 that computes a potential 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 such that in 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{\phi}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w(e)>-2W$
\end_inset

. It runs in expected time 
\begin_inset Formula $O(T_{r}(m,n))$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Construct 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 on the same vertices and edges with 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{H}(e)=\left\lceil w_{G}(e)/W\right\rceil +1$
\end_inset

. 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 is restricted: every weight is an integer and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{H}(e)\geq\left\lceil (-3W+1)/W\right\rceil +1\geq-1$
\end_inset

; and for every cycle 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 (which is nonnegative in 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

),
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{e\in C}\left(\left\lceil \frac{w(e)}{W}\right\rceil +1\right)\geq|C|+\sum_{e\in C}\frac{w(e)}{W}\geq|C|.
\]
\end_inset

The 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 buys the cycle slack. Compute (Restricted SSSP) a potential 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{H}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{H}(u,v)+\phi_{H}(u)-\phi_{H}(v)\geq0$
\end_inset

 and return 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi(v)=W\cdot\phi_{H}(v)$
\end_inset

. Since 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{H}(e)<\frac{w_{G}(e)}{W}+2$
\end_inset

 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{G}(e)>W\cdot(w_{H}(e)-2)$
\end_inset

, hence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w_{G_{\phi}}(u,v)=w_{G}(u,v)+W\phi_{H}(u)-W\phi_{H}(v)>-2W+W\big(w_{H}(u,v)+\phi_{H}(u)-\phi_{H}(v)\big)\geq-2W.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Algorithm

\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SSSP}(G)$
\end_inset

, for 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 without negative cycles and 
\begin_inset Formula $w(e)>-3W$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Multiply all weights by 
\begin_inset Formula $4n$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\begin_inset Formula $L=O(\log nW)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{0}\leftarrow G$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For 
\begin_inset Formula $i=0$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $L-1$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi\leftarrow\mathrm{Scale}(G_{i})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{i+1}\leftarrow(G_{i})_{\phi}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Round up to 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 all negative weights in 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{L}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Run Dijkstra on 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{L}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Claim
\begin_inset Argument 1
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
correctness
\end_layout

\end_inset

The algorithm is correct.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Each call to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{Scale}$
\end_inset

 reduces 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 (initially 
\begin_inset Formula $O(nW)$
\end_inset

 after the multiplication) by a factor of about 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{2}{3}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $-2W\geq-3W^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $W^{\prime}\approx\frac{2}{3}W$
\end_inset

), until 
\begin_inset Formula $W=2$
\end_inset

, i.e. all weights are 
\begin_inset Formula $>-6$
\end_inset

; the last iteration makes the weights 
\begin_inset Formula $>-4$
\end_inset

 (but 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 stays 2). Shortest paths in 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{L}$
\end_inset

 are the same as in 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

. Zeroing out the negative weights can increase the weight of a non-shortest path by at most 
\begin_inset Formula $3n$
\end_inset

, but after multiplying by 
\begin_inset Formula $4n$
\end_inset

 the difference between the shortest and the second-shortest path is at least 
\begin_inset Formula $4n$
\end_inset

. So a shortest path in the rounded graph is a shortest path in 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Running time: 
\begin_inset Formula $O(T_{r}(m,n)\log nW)=O(\mathrm{Dijkstra}(G)\cdot\log^{5}n\cdot\log nW)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Directed low-diameter decomposition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
\begin_inset Argument 1
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
LDD
\end_layout

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $G=(V,E,w)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w:E\to\mathbb{Z}_{\geq0}$
\end_inset

, be a directed nonnegatively-weighted graph. There is a randomized algorithm that computes a set of edges 
\begin_inset Formula $E^{-}$
\end_inset

 such that
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
every strongly connected component of 
\begin_inset Formula $G\setminus E^{-}$
\end_inset

 has weak diameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq d$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $e\in E^{-}$
\end_inset

 with probability at most 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(\log^{2}n\cdot\frac{w(e)}{d}+\frac{1}{n^{8}}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Weak diameter means distances are measured in all of 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 (paths may leave the component); strong diameter would measure them inside the component, which is larger, so weak diameter is the easier requirement. The point of the second property is that short edges are rarely cut, and long edges are cut with probability proportional to their weight. LDD in undirected graphs was known before; the twist of these algorithms is a directed version, and its use for restricted graphs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Restricted SSSP
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Topologically sort the strongly connected components 
\begin_inset Formula $(S_{1},\ldots,S_{k})$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $(V,E\setminus E^{-},w)$
\end_inset

. We may assume that the edges 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,v)\in E^{-}$
\end_inset

 go backwards: 
\begin_inset Formula $u\in S_{j}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in S_{i}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $j>i$
\end_inset

 (edges between components that go forward are not cut).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Notation: 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{\geq0}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 with negative weights replaced by 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $G[S]$
\end_inset

 is the subgraph induced by 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\ell,r}^{-}$
\end_inset

 is the set of edges between different components of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}_{\ell,r}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Algorithm

\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{RestrictedSSSP}(G)$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $L\leftarrow\lceil\log2n^{2}\rceil$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $R\leftarrow(2+c)\log n$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t\leftarrow4C\log^{2}n+1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{0}=\{0\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell\leftarrow1,\ldots,L$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
for 
\begin_inset Formula $r\leftarrow1,\ldots,R$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}_{\ell,r}\leftarrow(S_{1},\ldots,S_{k})=\mathrm{LDD}(G_{\geq0},2^{\ell})$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
for 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in[k]$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi\in\Phi_{\ell-1}$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{\phi}=\mathrm{BFD}(G_{\phi}[S_{i}],S_{i},t)$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
let 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi^{i}(v)\leftarrow\min_{\phi\in\Phi_{\ell-1}}d_{\phi}(v)$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
let 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\ell,r}(v)\leftarrow\phi^{i}(v)-i\cdot n$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in S_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{\ell}\leftarrow\sset{\phi_{\ell,r}}{r\in[R]}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Return 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{L,1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Think of a table with rows 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell=1,\ldots,L$
\end_inset

 and columns 
\begin_inset Formula $r=1,\ldots,R$
\end_inset

; each cell stores one LDD 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}_{\ell,r}$
\end_inset

 and one potential 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\ell,r}$
\end_inset

. Within a row the repetitions are independent, and the rows are computed bottom-up: cell 
\begin_inset Formula $(\ell,r)$
\end_inset

 runs BFD inside its components with every potential of row 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell-1$
\end_inset

, and takes the pointwise minimum.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The crucial property of the LDD
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 be a component of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{LDD}(G_{\geq0},d)$
\end_inset

 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 be a shortest path realizing 
\begin_inset Formula $d(S,v)$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $G[S]$
\end_inset

 (from the super-source of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

). Then 
\begin_inset Formula $|P|\leq d$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{\geq0}(P)\leq d$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 has weak diameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq d$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{\geq0}$
\end_inset

, there is a return path 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 (from the end of 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 back to its start) with 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{\geq0}(Q)\leq d$
\end_inset

. Then 
\begin_inset Formula $w(P+Q)\leq d$
\end_inset

, since 
\begin_inset Formula $w(P)\leq0$
\end_inset

. By the cycle condition of restricted graphs, 
\begin_inset Formula $|P|+|Q|\leq w(P+Q)\leq d$
\end_inset

. Finally, since 
\begin_inset Formula $w(e)\geq-1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{\geq0}(P)\leq w(P)+|P|\leq|P|\leq d$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Correctness
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim
\begin_inset Argument 1
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
main invariant
\end_layout

\end_inset

For all 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell,r$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e\in E\setminus E_{\ell,r}^{-}$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{\phi_{\ell,r}}(e)\geq0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $L=\lceil\log2n^{2}\rceil$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{L}\geq2n^{2}\geq(n-1)(n-2)$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{L,1}^{-}=\emptyset$
\end_inset

, and the invariant gives that 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{L,1}$
\end_inset

 is a valid potential: the algorithm is correct.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The bad event 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\ell,r,v}$
\end_inset

 (for level 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset

, repetition 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 and vertex 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

): fix 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 and a vertex 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

, let 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 be the component of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}_{\ell,r}=\mathrm{LDD}(G_{\geq0},2^{\ell})$
\end_inset

 containing 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 realize 
\begin_inset Formula $d(S,v)$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $G[S]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
By the crucial property, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{\geq0}(P)\leq2^{\ell}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Fix some 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}_{\ell-1,r^{\prime}}$
\end_inset

. An edge 
\begin_inset Formula $e\in P$
\end_inset

 is in 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\ell-1,r^{\prime}}^{-}$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq C\log^{2}n\cdot\frac{w_{\geq0}(e)}{2^{\ell-1}}+O\left(\frac{1}{n^{8}}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
So the expected number of edges of 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\ell-1,r^{\prime}}^{-}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq C\log^{2}n\cdot\frac{w_{\geq0}(P)}{2^{\ell-1}}+O\left(\frac{1}{n^{7}}\right)\leq2C\log^{2}n+O\left(\frac{1}{n^{7}}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
By Markov, with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 the number of edges of 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\ell-1,r^{\prime}}^{-}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq t=4C\log^{2}n+1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The repetitions are independent, so with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{R}$
\end_inset

 this happens for all 
\begin_inset Formula $r^{\prime}\in[R]$
\end_inset

. This is the bad event 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\ell,r,v}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Union bound over all 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell,r,v$
\end_inset

: some 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\ell,r,v}$
\end_inset

 happens with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq n\log^{2}n\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{R}\leq\frac{1}{n^{c}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assume from now on that no bad event happens. We prove the main invariant by induction, via the two statements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A(\ell):\ \forall r,S_{i}\in\mathcal{S}_{\ell,r},v\in S_{i}:\quad\phi^{i}(v)\ \Big(=\min_{\phi\in\Phi_{\ell-1}}d_{\phi}(v)\Big)=d_{G[S_{i}]}(S_{i},v),
\]
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B(\ell):\ \forall r,e\in E\setminus E_{\ell,r}^{-}:\quad w_{\phi_{\ell,r}}(e)\geq0.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim
\begin_inset Argument 1
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
base case
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $A(1)$
\end_inset

 holds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 be the path realizing 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{G[S_{i}]}(S_{i},v)$
\end_inset

. In level 1 the components have weak diameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq2$
\end_inset

, so by the crucial property 
\begin_inset Formula $|P|\leq2$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{\phi}=\mathrm{BFD}(G_{\phi}[S_{i}],S_{i},t)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi=0$
\end_inset

 finds it. Hence 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi^{i}(v)=d_{\phi}(v)=d_{G[S_{i}]}(S_{i},v)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim

\begin_inset Formula $A(\ell)\Rightarrow B(\ell)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Fix 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 and an edge 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,v)\in E\setminus E_{\ell,r}^{-}$
\end_inset

. If 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 are in the same 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{i}\in\mathcal{S}_{\ell,r}$
\end_inset

, this follows from 
\begin_inset Formula $A(\ell)$
\end_inset

 (distances satisfy the triangle inequality, and the shift by 
\begin_inset Formula $-i\cdot n$
\end_inset

 is the same for 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

). Otherwise 
\begin_inset Formula $u\in S_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in S_{j}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $i<j$
\end_inset

 (the edge is not cut, so it goes forward), and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w_{\phi_{\ell,r}}(e)=w(e)+\phi^{i}(u)-i\cdot n-\big(\phi^{j}(v)-j\cdot n\big)\geq-1-(n-1)+0+(j-i)\cdot n\geq0,
\]
\end_inset

using 
\begin_inset Formula $A(\ell)$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi^{i}(u)=d_{G[S_{i}]}(S_{i},u)\geq-(n-1)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi^{j}(v)=d_{G[S_{j}]}(S_{j},v)\leq0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim

\begin_inset Formula $B(\ell)\Rightarrow A(\ell+1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Fix 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{i}\in\mathcal{S}_{\ell+1,r}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in S_{i}$
\end_inset

, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 be the path realizing 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{G[S_{i}]}(S_{i},v)$
\end_inset

. Since 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\ell+1,r,v}$
\end_inset

 does not happen, there is 
\begin_inset Formula $r^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 has 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq t=4C\log^{2}n+1$
\end_inset

 edges of 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\ell,r^{\prime}}^{-}$
\end_inset

. By 
\begin_inset Formula $B(\ell)$
\end_inset

, under 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\ell,r^{\prime}}\in\Phi_{\ell}$
\end_inset

 all other edges of 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 are nonnegative, so 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 has at most 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 negative edges in 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{\phi_{\ell,r^{\prime}}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{\phi}=\mathrm{BFD}(G_{\phi_{\ell,r^{\prime}}}[S_{i}],S_{i},t)$
\end_inset

 finds it: 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{\phi}(v)=d_{G[S_{i}]}(S_{i},v)$
\end_inset

. The minimum over 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{\ell}$
\end_inset

 can only be smaller, and it is never below the true distance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Running time and Las Vegas
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Each BFD takes 
\begin_inset Formula $O((m_{i}+n_{i}\log n_{i})\log^{2}n)$
\end_inset

 time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell,r$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum m_{i}\leq m$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum n_{i}\leq n$
\end_inset

, so one BFD for 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell,r$
\end_inset

 (over all components) takes 
\begin_inset Formula $O((m+n\log n)\log^{2}n)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We do 
\begin_inset Formula $|\Phi_{\ell-1}|=\log n$
\end_inset

 of these for each 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell,r$
\end_inset

, in total 
\begin_inset Formula $O((m+n\log n)\log^{3}n)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
There are 
\begin_inset Formula $\log^{2}n$
\end_inset

 pairs 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell,r$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $O((m+n\log n)\log^{5}n)$
\end_inset

 time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Each LDD takes 
\begin_inset Formula $O(m\log^{2}n)$
\end_inset

, so the total LDD time is 
\begin_inset Formula $O(m\log^{4}n)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We showed an algorithm that succeeds with high probability. Testing whether a potential is valid takes 
\begin_inset Formula $O(m)$
\end_inset

 time, so under the assumption that there is no negative cycle we can easily turn it into a Las Vegas algorithm: check the result and repeat if it fails.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Low-diameter decomposition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Undirected graphs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Algorithm
Undirected LDD
\begin_inset Formula $(G,d)$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $E^{-}\leftarrow\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
While 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 is not empty:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
pick an arbitrary vertex 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
sample 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{v}\sim\mathrm{Geo}(p)$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $p\leftarrow\min\left\{ 1,\frac{20\ln n}{d}\right\}$
\end_inset

; if 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{v}>\frac{d}{2}$
\end_inset

 set 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{v}\leftarrow\frac{d}{2}$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $E^{-}\leftarrow E^{-}\cup\partial B(v,R_{v})$
\end_inset

 (this makes the ball 
\begin_inset Formula $B(v,R_{v})$
\end_inset

 a connected component);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $G\leftarrow G\setminus B(v,R_{v})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Return 
\begin_inset Formula $E^{-}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Every component is a ball of radius 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq\frac{d}{2}$
\end_inset

, so its diameter is 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq d$
\end_inset

. In the lecture we ran it on a grid with 
\begin_inset Formula $n=48$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $d=100$
\end_inset

: each round grows a random ball, removes it with its boundary edges, and continues on what is left.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim
\begin_inset Argument 1
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
second property
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\Pr(e\in E^{-})=O\left(\frac{w(e)\cdot\log n}{d}+\frac{1}{n^{9}}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Consider an edge 
\begin_inset Formula $e=(u,v)$
\end_inset

 of weight 
\begin_inset Formula $w(e)$
\end_inset

 and the first ball that reaches 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

, say w.l.o.g. 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

. The geometric distribution is memoryless, so the probability that the ball stops within the next 
\begin_inset Formula $w(e)$
\end_inset

 coin tosses (and cuts 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

) is 
\begin_inset Formula $1-(1-p)^{w(e)}\leq w(e)\cdot p=O\left(\frac{w(e)\log n}{d}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
It could also be that we truncate a radius to 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{d}{2}$
\end_inset

 (and cut the edge). But 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pr\left(\mathrm{Geo}(p)>\frac{d}{2}\right)\leq(1-p)^{d/2}\leq e^{-pd/2}=e^{-10\ln n}=\frac{1}{n^{10}}$
\end_inset

, so by a union bound 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pr(\text{some ball is truncated})\leq\frac{1}{n^{9}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Directed graphs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 is directed with nonnegative weights, and we need strongly connected components of weak diameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq d$
\end_inset

. 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{in}(v,r)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{out}(v,r)$
\end_inset

 are the in- and out-balls (vertices at distance 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq r$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

, from 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

), and 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial B_{in}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial B_{out}$
\end_inset

 are the edges entering, resp. leaving, the ball.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Algorithm
Directed LDD
\begin_inset Formula $(G,d)$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $E^{-}\leftarrow\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Mark 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 in-light if 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|B_{in}\left(v,\frac{d}{2}\right)\right|\leq\frac{n}{2}$
\end_inset

, and out-light if 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|B_{out}\left(v,\frac{d}{2}\right)\right|\leq\frac{n}{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
While 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 contains a vertex 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 marked in/out-light:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
sample 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{v}\sim\mathrm{Geo}(p)$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $p\leftarrow\min\left\{ 1,\frac{20\ln n}{d}\right\}$
\end_inset

; if 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{v}>\frac{d}{2}$
\end_inset

 set 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{v}\leftarrow\frac{d}{2}$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $E^{-}\leftarrow E^{-}\cup\partial B_{in/out}(v,R_{v})\cup\mathrm{LDD}\big(B_{in/out}(v,R_{v})\big)$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $G\leftarrow G\setminus B(v,R_{v})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Return 
\begin_inset Formula $E^{-}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We may assume the graph is strongly connected (otherwise work on each component separately).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The main differences from the undirected case: separation into in- and out-balls, and carving only balls of light vertices, using recursion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Since a carved ball has at most 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{n}{2}$
\end_inset

 vertices, the depth of the recursion is 
\begin_inset Formula $\log n$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the example from the lecture (
\begin_inset Formula $d=100$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $p=\frac{20\ln28}{100}\approx\frac{2}{3}$
\end_inset

) the graph has a heavy core 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 and two light parts: the out-ball 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{out}(B0,2)$
\end_inset

 is exactly the 8 vertices of 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cup B$
\end_inset

 and its leaving edges are cut, then 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{in}(Q0,1)$
\end_inset

 is carved, and the recursive call on 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cup B$
\end_inset

 carves an in-ball around 
\begin_inset Formula $B2$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim
The heavy vertices that remain have weak diameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq d$
\end_inset

, and are strongly connected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 be heavy. Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|B_{out}\left(u,\frac{d}{2}\right)\right|>\frac{n}{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|B_{in}\left(v,\frac{d}{2}\right)\right|>\frac{n}{2}$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{out}(u)\cap B_{in}(v)\neq\emptyset$
\end_inset

: there is 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 in both, and a path 
\begin_inset Formula $u\to w\to v$
\end_inset

 of weight 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq d$
\end_inset

 (note that 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 itself may be light). A strongly connected component in a recursive call is also strongly connected at the top level, so all components have weak diameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq d$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim
\begin_inset Argument 1
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
cutting probability
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\Pr(e\in E^{-})=O\left(\frac{w(e)\log^{2}n}{d}+\frac{1}{n^{8}}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
In a single LDD the same analysis as in the undirected case gives 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(\frac{w(e)\log n}{d}+\frac{1}{n^{9}}\right)$
\end_inset

. But now an edge participates in 
\begin_inset Formula $\log n$
\end_inset

 LDDs (the recursion depth), and a union bound gives the claim.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Running time and heavy-light classification
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Carving out the balls takes 
\begin_inset Formula $O(m+n\log n)$
\end_inset

, since each edge is scanned by at most two Dijkstra computations. The bottleneck is classifying the vertices into heavy and light, which we do by sampling.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Algorithm
Heavy-light classification:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Sample 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{1},\ldots,v_{\ell}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell=c\log n$
\end_inset

, and compute 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{in}\left(v_{i},\frac{d}{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{out}\left(v_{i},\frac{d}{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,\ell$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For a vertex 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and the sample 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|B_{out}\left(x,\frac{d}{2}\right)\cap S\right|=\left|\sset{i\in[\ell]}{x\in B_{in}\left(v_{i},\frac{d}{2}\right)}\right|$
\end_inset

, and symmetrically for 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{in}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\ell}\left|B_{out}\left(x,\frac{d}{2}\right)\cap S\right|\leq0.6$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is out-light; if 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\ell}\left|B_{in}\left(x,\frac{d}{2}\right)\cap S\right|\leq0.6$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is in-light.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}=1$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}\in B_{out}\left(x,\frac{d}{2}\right)\cap S$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 otherwise, so 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pr(X_{i}=1)=\frac{|B_{out}(x,d/2)|}{n}$
\end_inset

. Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{avg}=\frac{1}{\ell}\sum X_{i}$
\end_inset

; then 
\begin_inset Formula $E[X_{avg}]=\frac{|B_{out}(x,d/2)|}{n}$
\end_inset

, and by Hoeffding 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pr(|X_{avg}-E[X_{avg}]|\geq\epsilon)\leq2e^{-2\ell\epsilon^{2}}$
\end_inset

. With 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=0.1$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq2e^{-0.02\ell}\leq\frac{1}{n^{10}}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell=c\log n$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 large enough, so with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $1-O\left(\frac{1}{n^{9}}\right)$
\end_inset

 this bad event does not happen for any ball. Assume it does not:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is neither out-light nor in-light, both ratios are 
\begin_inset Formula $>0.6$
\end_inset

, hence 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{|B_{in}(x,d/2)|}{n}>0.5$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{|B_{out}(x,d/2)|}{n}>0.5$
\end_inset

, which is what we need for the heavy vertices to be strongly connected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is (say) in-light, the ratio is 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq0.6$
\end_inset

, hence 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{|B_{in}(x,d/2)|}{n}<0.7$
\end_inset

. So the recursion is still on components that are a constant fraction smaller, and the depth is still 
\begin_inset Formula $O(\log n)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Running time: 2 Dijkstra runs per sampled vertex, 
\begin_inset Formula $O((m+n\log n)\log n)$
\end_inset

 per level; there are 
\begin_inset Formula $O(\log n)$
\end_inset

 recursive levels and the problems in each level are disjoint, so the total is 
\begin_inset Formula $O((m+n\log n)\log^{2}n)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
A strongly polynomial algorithm for dense graphs (LLRZ)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Li, Li, Rao and Zhang (STOC 2026), "Shortcutting for negative-weight shortest paths", give an 
\begin_inset Formula $O(n^{2.5})$
\end_inset

-type strongly polynomial algorithm (no dependence on the weights). This part continues next lecture; these notes cover it up to the balancing of the searches.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Setup and main idea
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We may assume every vertex has at most one negative outgoing edge: replace the negative edges 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,v_{1}),(u,v_{2})$
\end_inset

 of weights 
\begin_inset Formula $-6,-2$
\end_inset

 by an edge 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,u^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 of weight 
\begin_inset Formula $-6$
\end_inset

 and edges 
\begin_inset Formula $(u^{\prime},v_{1}),(u^{\prime},v_{2})$
\end_inset

 of weights 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $4$
\end_inset

. A vertex is negative (
\begin_inset Formula $\in N$
\end_inset

) if it has a negative outgoing edge; denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $k=|N|$
\end_inset

 the number of negative vertices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Reduce the number of negative edges on a shortest path by a constant factor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If we can do it in subcubic time, then after 
\begin_inset Formula $O(\log k)$
\end_inset

 iterations we can finish with BFD.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
LLRZ obtain a faster algorithm by controlling the number of vertices added and using recursion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Naive ambition: turn two negative hops into one. For negative edges 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,u^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(r,r^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 add the shortcut 
\begin_inset Formula $w(u,r^{\prime})=w(u,u^{\prime})+d^{0}(u^{\prime},r)+w(r,r^{\prime})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $d^{0}$
\end_inset

 is the distance using nonnegative edges only. There are at most 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{2}$
\end_inset

 candidate shortcuts, but computing their weights requires nonnegative-path distances: Dijkstra from each of the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 negative-edge heads, 
\begin_inset Formula $O(k\cdot(m+n\log n))=\Omega(n^{3})$
\end_inset

 for dense graphs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
3-to-2 shortcutting
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
LLRZ use a weaker goal: replace three consecutive negative edges 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,u^{\prime}),(r,r^{\prime}),(v,v^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 on a shortest path by two, with either a backward direct shortcut 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,r^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 of weight 
\begin_inset Formula $w(u,u^{\prime})+d^{0}(u^{\prime},r)+w(r,r^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 or a forward direct shortcut 
\begin_inset Formula $(r,v^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 of weight 
\begin_inset Formula $d^{-}(r,v)+w(v,v^{\prime})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $d^{-}(r,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is the distance from 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 starting with the negative edge 
\begin_inset Formula $(r,r^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 and continuing with nonnegative edges. Computing all of these is too expensive, so we truncate the searches.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
For each 
\begin_inset Formula $r\in N$
\end_inset

 with negative edge 
\begin_inset Formula $(r,r^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 we compute a threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{r}$
\end_inset

 and sets 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{in}(r),V_{out}(r)$
\end_inset

 such that
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $v\in V_{in}(r)\Rightarrow d^{0}(v,r)\leq\Delta_{r}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $v\notin V_{in}(r)\Rightarrow d^{0}(v,r)\geq\Delta_{r}$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $v\in V_{out}(r)\Rightarrow d^{-}(r,v)\leq-\Delta_{r}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $v\notin V_{out}(r)\Rightarrow d^{-}(r,v)\geq-\Delta_{r}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
The direct shortcuts:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
add a direct forward shortcut 
\begin_inset Formula $(r,v^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in V_{out}(r)$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $(v,v^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is negative;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
add a direct backward shortcut 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,r^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{\prime}\in V_{in}(r)\cup\{r\}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,u^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is negative.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The direct shortcuts are not sufficient: the path may enter 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{in}(r)$
\end_inset

 and leave 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{out}(r)$
\end_inset

 in the middle of its nonnegative parts. So we add a Steiner node 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{r}$
\end_inset

 and Steiner shortcuts:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}\in V_{in}(r)\cup\{r\}$
\end_inset

 and edges 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{1},x_{1})$
\end_inset

: if 
\begin_inset Formula $w(y_{1},x_{1})+d^{0}(x_{1},r)-\Delta_{r}\geq0$
\end_inset

, add 
\begin_inset Formula $w(y_{1},\tilde{r})=w(y_{1},x_{1})+d^{0}(x_{1},r)-\Delta_{r}$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}\in V_{out}(r)\cup\{r\}$
\end_inset

 and edges 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{2},y_{2})$
\end_inset

: if 
\begin_inset Formula $d^{-}(r,x_{2})+w(x_{2},y_{2})+\Delta_{r}\geq0$
\end_inset

, add 
\begin_inset Formula $w(\tilde{r},y_{2})=d^{-}(r,x_{2})+w(x_{2},y_{2})+\Delta_{r}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim
\begin_inset Argument 1
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
correctness
\end_layout

\end_inset

A shortest path with 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 negative edges becomes a path with 
\begin_inset Formula $h-\lfloor h/3\rfloor$
\end_inset

 negative edges.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Look at three consecutive negative edges 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,u^{\prime}),(r,r^{\prime}),(v,v^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 on a shortest path. If 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{\prime}\in V_{in}(r)\cup\{r\}$
\end_inset

, use the direct backward shortcut. If 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in V_{out}(r)$
\end_inset

, use the direct forward shortcut. Otherwise let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 be the first vertex of the path in 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{in}(r)\cup\{r\}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 the last vertex in 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{out}(r)\cup\{r\}$
\end_inset

, with 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

 the vertex before 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 the vertex after 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

; we added 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{1},\tilde{r})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(\tilde{r},y_{2})$
\end_inset

, both nonnegative. In every case the three negative edges are replaced by two.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
How many shortcuts do we add?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{r}=|V_{in}(r)|$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{r}=|V_{out}(r)|$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Direct shortcuts: 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq a_{r}+b_{r}+1=O(a_{r}+b_{r}+1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Steiner shortcuts: 
\begin_inset Formula $O(n(a_{r}+b_{r}+1))$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Time to find these shortcuts: 
\begin_inset Formula $O(n(a_{r}+b_{r}+1)+(a_{r}+b_{r}+1)\log n)=O(n(a_{r}+b_{r}+1))$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
So we want to minimize 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(nk+n\sum_{r\in N}(a_{r}+b_{r})\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A large 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{r}$
\end_inset

 makes 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{in}(r)$
\end_inset

 large and 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{out}(r)$
\end_inset

 small, and a small 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{r}$
\end_inset

 the opposite. We will pick 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{r}$
\end_inset

 so that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\begin_inset Formula $|a_{r}-b_{r}|\leq1$
\end_inset

, and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
for all 
\begin_inset Formula $u\in V_{in}(r)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in V_{out}(r)$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $d^{0}(u,r)+d^{-}(r,v)<0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Betweenness reduction: we will bound 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{r\in N}a_{r}b_{r}\leq\frac{kn^{2}}{b}$
\end_inset

. This suffices, since 
\begin_inset Formula $(a_{r}+b_{r})^{2}=(a_{r}-b_{r})^{2}+4a_{r}b_{r}\leq1+4a_{r}b_{r}$
\end_inset

, so
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{r\in N}(a_{r}+b_{r})^{2}\leq k+4\sum_{r\in N}a_{r}b_{r}=O\left(\frac{kn^{2}}{b}\right),\qquad\sum_{r\in N}(a_{r}+b_{r})\leq\sqrt{k\sum_{r\in N}(a_{r}+b_{r})^{2}}=O\left(\frac{kn}{\sqrt{b}}\right),
\]
\end_inset

and in total 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(nk+n\sum_{r\in N}(a_{r}+b_{r})\right)=O\left(\frac{kn^{2}}{\sqrt{b}}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two things remain: how to guarantee 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{r\in N}a_{r}b_{r}\leq\frac{kn^{2}}{b}$
\end_inset

 (betweenness reduction), and how to balance the forward and backward searches to satisfy the two conditions above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Balancing the searches
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Run in tandem a backward search from 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 in the nonnegative subgraph, and a forward search from 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 starting with the negative edge 
\begin_inset Formula $(r,r^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 and continuing in the nonnegative subgraph, with two separate priority queues. Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 be the minimum unsettled backward key and 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 the minimum unsettled forward key; initially 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f=w(r,r^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 (the keys of 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r^{\prime}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Algorithm
Repeat:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
if 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta+f\geq0$
\end_inset

, stop; otherwise settle a vertex in the backward search;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
if 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta+f\geq0$
\end_inset

, stop; otherwise settle a vertex in the forward search.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Setting 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{r}$
\end_inset

: if we stop after a forward step, set 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{r}=\beta$
\end_inset

; if we stop after a backward step, set 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{r}=-f$
\end_inset

. The settled vertices form 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{in}(r)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{out}(r)$
\end_inset

. The proof that these choices satisfy the two guarantees, and the betweenness reduction, are left for the next lecture.
\end_layout

